Decide whether work is actually needed. Producing a plan is the expected outcome —
assume the work is real and needed. Closing an item without a plan is an EXCEPTION
that demands proof; it is NOT a shortcut to avoid work. For the cases below, use the
tracker MCP, set a clear closing summary, then STOP — do NOT produce a plan. (Never
set "finalized" yourself — the tracker finalizes the item automatically once it
reaches a terminal status; finalization is not the agent's decision.)
- ALREADY IMPLEMENTED / NO WORK NEEDED → take this ONLY with concrete PROOF that the
  work is already complete or genuinely unnecessary: you located the exact change in
  the tree, the bug provably no longer reproduces, or another item/commit already did
  it. Quote the proof (commit SHA, item ID, or the code) in the summary. Do NOT close
  because the task merely looks done or to save effort — if you cannot prove it, plan
  the work. Set the matching terminal status (NEVER "done", which means THIS flow
  implemented and merged the work):
    - duplicate          → already done by another item/commit
    - cant_reproduce     → the bug provably no longer reproduces / is already fixed
    - works_as_intended  → current behavior already matches what the item asks
- UNSURE whether the work is needed, or how to proceed → do NOT guess and do NOT
  close. Ask the user via mcp__tracker__ask_user_question (never plain text), then stop.
- NOT FEASIBLE as specified → set status wontfix with a clear summary, and add the
  "needs-attention" tag.
